ns. The current code only tiles inner j loop. We could also tile outer i loop to reuse loaded x[j], y[j], z[j] across multiple i iterations within a block. That is 2D tiling: for (ii=0; ii<n; ii+=TILE) { for (jj=0; jj<n; jj+=TILE) { ... } }. This would improve cache reuse of the x,y,z arrays when iterating over i and j within a tile. However, we must be careful about accumulation order: forces are accumulated per i, so if we reorder loops, we need to ensure that for each i, all j contributions are added exactly once in original order? Actually 2D tiling changes the order of additions because inner loop over j is broken into tiles and outer i also tiled. The reference sequential code does: for i=0..n-1 { fx=fy=fz=0; for j=0..n-1 { compute force from j on i } update vx[i] }. So the order of additions to fx,fy,fz is by increasing j. If we tile both loops, we might process some j contributions later than others but still all contributions are added exactly once per i. However, due to floating-point non-associativity, different summation order can produce slightly different results. The correctness check likely compares output bit-for-bit against reference sequential code (which uses the exact same operations in the same order). So any change in operation order will cause mismatch.\n\nThus we must preserve the exact accumulation order: for each i, j runs from 0 to n-1 sequentially. Tiling only inner loop with tiles that are contiguous and processed in increasing j order preserves order because within a tile, j increases; across tiles, j also increases (since jj increments by TILE_SIZE). So current tiling is safe.\n\n2D tiling would change the order of i iterations? Actually if we tile outer i as well, then for each ii block, we process all j blocks. But inside an ii block, we still have inner loop over j in increasing order? The typical 2D tiling: for (ii=0; ii<n; ii+=TILE) { for (jj=0; jj<n; jj+=TILE) { for (i=ii; i<min(ii+TILE,n); ++i) { compute forces from bodies in [